
Review System Journal Entries
journalctl - To retrieve log messages from the journal
-----------------example------------------
journalctl
journalctl -n 5 = show 5 last log entries
journalctl -f = display last 10 lines and continues output journal when the journal appends.
filter the output of the journal by the priority of the journal entries. 
journalctl command processes the debug, info, notice, warning, err, crit, alert, and emerg priority levels, in ascending priority order.
journalctl -p err = ist journal entries with the err priority or higher
journalctl -u sshd.service =  for a specified systemd unit by using the journalctl command -u option and the unit name
journalctl --since today = timestamp. yesterday, today, and tomorrow 
journalctl --since "2022-03-11 20:30" --until "2022-03-14 10:00"
journalctl -o verbose =  verbose output is useful to reduce the output of complex searches for certain events in the journal.
journalctl _SYSTEMD_UNIT=sshd.service _PID=2110 = journalctl command shows all related journal entries to the sshd.service systemd unit from a process with PID 2110
